      "source": [
        "# Efficient Diffusion Models — Reproduction Experiment\n",
        "\n",
        "**Course:** Deep Learning  \n",
        "**Assignment:** Short Story Assignment  \n",
        "**Student:** Shilpa Yelkur Ramakrishnaiah  \n",
        "**Paper Reviewed:** *Efficient Diffusion Models: A Survey* — TMLR 2025\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objective\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook provides a lightweight reproduction experiment for the short story assignment.  \n",
        "The goal is to reproduce one of the core findings discussed in the survey paper and presentation:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Reducing the number of diffusion sampling steps can significantly reduce inference time, but very low step counts may reduce visual quality. After a moderate number of steps, quality improvements usually become smaller compared to the additional compute cost.\n",
        "\n",
        "## What This Notebook Tests\n",
        "\n",
        "Using Stable Diffusion with a DDIM scheduler, this notebook generates the same prompt using different inference step counts:\n",
        "\n",
        "- 10 steps\n",
        "- 20 steps\n",
        "- 30 steps\n",
        "- 50 steps\n",
        "\n",
        "For each setting, the notebook records:\n",
        "\n",
        "- inference time\n",
        "- generated image output\n",
        "- qualitative visual comparison\n",
        "- latency trend across step counts\n",
        "\n",
        "This directly supports the assignment requirement to reproduce something from the selected paper using an experiment-style workflow."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "216059e8",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "216059e8"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Why This Experiment Matches the Paper\n",
        "\n",
        "The selected survey paper discusses efficient diffusion models across algorithm-level, system-level, and framework-level optimization. One major algorithm-level direction is reducing the number of Neural Function Evaluations (NFEs), meaning fewer denoising steps during sampling.\n",
        "\n",
        "Methods such as DDIM, DPM-Solver, consistency models, and progressive distillation are all designed to reduce sampling cost while preserving acceptable generation quality.\n",
        "\n",
        "This notebook focuses on the simplest reproducible version of that idea:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Compare diffusion outputs at different sampling step counts using the same model, same prompt, and same random seed.\n",
        "\n",
        "This is intentionally lightweight because the assignment asks for a practical reproduction, not full model training from scratch."
      ]
    },
